Z||000005||LAMBO||05-02-2000||12:44 PM||||I'm located in south central Iowa, about an hour south of Des Moines. You guys are right about getting hooked on these cars, I'm stopping by a place tonight to see if I can make a deal on trading my '95 Ford Aspire on an '86 GT. 84,000, all options, good to excellent cond. What do you all think I should offer the guy in trade. My car has 75,000, Excellent shape, extra set of winter snow tires, has summer tires and alloy wheels on now.<P>Also, on my commute back and forth to work, there is an '84 2m4 with a bad motor, fair cond., most options, asking $400 sitting out next to the road. I might offer $250, what do you think? Parts car? ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Old Lar||05-02-2000||12:54 PM||||Have the guy pay you $1000 for the Aspire and you'll take the old Pontiac off his hands. Then you'll have $ for the one with the bad engine.<P>Some things to consider is what is the condition of the other car components.  If the interior and body is in good shape and most of the mechanics are good, suspension, brakes, transmission, space age frame etc., it may be a car in need of an engine transplant or rebuild.  I don't know what an iron duke goes for.  Lots of options or as you suggest a parts car.||||reg||10
Z||000007||LAMBO||05-02-2000||01:50 PM||||Hey, I like how you think Old Lar. It's doubtful that he'll cough up any change for my Ford, but I'm hoping he doesn't know what he's got. I was thinking my car and $100 for his trouble. I'll let you know tomorrow.<P>As for the other car ('84, bad motor) it's mainly cosmetic stuff that's easy to fix, besides the motor thing. It has 3 or 4 things I can use on my 85 that will easily recoup my money, even at $400. The rest will be gravey. Who knows, it might get a V8 and a nose job, if I buy it, that is. ||||reg||12
